umbnail url="https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6aff832311ded6aaebf0e563/master/pass/image%20(5).png" width="1594" height="1598"/></item><item><title>“No Prophet,” by Nasser Rabah</title><link>https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2026/11/30/no-prophet-nasser-rabah-poem</link><guid isPermaLink="false">6afc66f54b856176dd13076f</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:00:00 +0000</pubDate><media:content/><description>“I’ve been running since running came / into being.”</description><category>Magazine / Poems</category><dc:creator>Nasser Rabah</dc:creator><dc:publisher>Condé Nast</dc:publisher><media:thumbnail url="https://media.newyorker.com/photos/5d484c4743af05000896cd25/master/pass/hp-poetryspots1.jpg" width="1200" height="900"/></item><item><title>The Mystery of Mercury</title><link>https://www.newyorker.com/science/elements/the-mystery-of-mercury</link><guid isPermaLink="false">6afdcaad14cfb57caf11782a</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:00:00 +0000</pubDate><media:content/><description>Its surface can be hotter than an oven—yet its craters contain ice that may be older than life as we know it. An innovative spacecraft, BepiColombo, just entered the planet’s orbit.</description><category>Science / Elements</category><media:keywords>Mercury, Space, Astronomy, Space Exploration</media:keywords><dc:creator>David W. Brown</dc:creator><dc:publisher>Condé Nast</dc:publisher><media:thumbnail url="https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6b0079ad565d1b36aafe5b06/master/pass/new-yorker-mercury-hi-res.jpg" width="2560" height="1440"/></item><item><title>Play Catalogues: Sunday, November 22, 2026</title><link>https://www.newyorker.com/puzzles-and-games-dept/catalogues/2026/11/22</link><guid isPermaLink="false">6aedef7b87180c777feb5ce6</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:00:00 +0000</pubDate><media:content/><description>Can you sort the items into the correct order?</description><category> Puzzles &amp; Games Dept. / Catalogues</category><dc:creator>Kate Chin Park</dc:creator><dc:publisher>Condé Nast</dc:publisher><media:thumbnail url="https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6a038e1598355286cc52147c/master/pass/Catalogues%2016-9-optimised.gif" width="1920" height="1080"/></item><item><title>“Chickenpox,” by Jane Shore</title><link>https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2026/11/30/chickenpox-jane-shore-poem</link><guid isPermaLink="false">6afc66fc6605b3baa195e43c</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:00:00 +0000</pubDate><media:content/><description>“When the fever broke, Ellen phoned; / her daughter was still contagious.”</description><category>Magazine / Poems</category><dc:creator>Jane Shore</dc:creator><dc:publisher>Condé Nast</dc:publisher><media:thumbnail url="https://media.newyorker.com/photos/5d484bf0773ec90008696e12/master/pass/hp-poetryspots2.jpg" width="1200" height="900"/></item><item><title>“Out of Time,” by Mary Gaitskill</title><link>https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2026/11/30/out-of-time-fiction-mary-gaitskill</link><guid isPermaLink="false">6afc6700c5413a72a000ae1a</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:00:00 +0000</pubDate><media:content/><description>It occurred to her that he, too, might want affection, to be embraced and held with sincerity; most people did.</description><category>Magazine / Fiction</category><dc:creator>Mary Gaitskill</dc:creator><dc:publisher>Condé Nast</dc:publisher><media:thumbnail url="https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6afc73c6272bf23468f4738b/master/pass/r50666.jpg" width="1707" height="2560"/></item></channel></rss>